## Dependency Impact (blocking)

Adding `File::FNM_EXTGLOB | File::FNM_PATHNAME` to `File.fnmatch?` (plan unit 1) is verified correct for the brief's own glob idiom (`/tags/**/*`, `/error/**/*`: confirmed by direct `File.fnmatch?` probing to match the directory, its index, and nested paths). But it silently changes the semantics of the *existing* default exclude glob `/assets/**` (no trailing `/*`), which today (without `FNM_PATHNAME`) matches every nested path under `/assets/`. Under the new flags, `File.fnmatch?("/assets/**", "/assets/nested/note.md", flags)` returns `false` (verified) — a one-level-deep-only match. This is a real regression, not a hypothetical:

- `spec/census_spec.rb:56` ("includes pages and posts and skips other collections by default") currently passes only because `/assets/**` drops `assets/nested/note.md` from the default `base_files` fixture (verified by running that example against `HEAD`). After the flag change with no other edit, that fixture page would newly survive the exclude filter and the example's `eq(["_posts/2020-01-02-hello.md", "about.md"])` assertion would fail.
- `spec/configuration_spec.rb:110` hardcodes `default_exclude = [..., "/assets/**"]` and asserts `Configuration#exclude` returns exactly that list — unaffected in isolation, but it pins the exact string that must change if the fix below is adopted.
- `README.md:63` documents the same default exclude value.

The plan's own Challenges & Mitigations for unit 1 says only "keep the existing exclude examples and run `spec/census_spec.rb` after the flag change" — that mitigation describes *observing* the break, not fixing it. No unit proposes changing `Configuration::DEFAULT_EXCLUDE`.

Fix (verified to restore existing behavior under the new flags): migrate the shipped default from `/assets/**` to `/assets/**/*` in `lib/jekyll/llms_txt/configuration.rb`, `README.md`, and `spec/configuration_spec.rb`'s `default_exclude` let. Re-verified with `File.fnmatch?("/assets/**/*", "/assets/nested/note.md", flags) #=> true` and the sibling one-level and index cases, so this migration is a drop-in replacement for the existing coverage. Add this as a step under plan unit 1, touching those three files, before the flag change lands.

## Completeness (fixable)

`spec/generator_spec.rb:162` ("writes sidecars and joins corpus bodies with one newline") builds its expected root-corpus string as `[...seven bodies ending in "\n"...].join("\n")`. Verified by direct simulation: under the old join, that produces a string ending in a trailing `\n` (the last body's own newline, untouched); under plan unit 2's chomp-then-`join("\n\n")`, the trailing newline is stripped, so the two computations diverge only at the very end (`"...Garden body.\n"` vs `"...Garden body."`) — confirmed unequal. Plan unit 2 only lists two examples to update (the `"A\n\nB\n"` and `"A\nB"` ones at lines 195 and 208); this third existing example is an affected consumer that unit 2's step list omits. Add updating this example's expected value (and its stale title, which still says "with one newline") to unit 2's stub-tests step.

## Radical Innovation (advisory, not applied)

The root cause of the Dependency Impact finding is that the fix ties correct recursive-exclude behavior to a specific glob spelling (`X/**/*`), so every existing and future default or site-configured glob written as bare `X/**` silently degrades instead of erroring or matching. A more accretive fix: in `Census#excluded?`, when a configured glob ends in `/**` with no trailing segment, also probe it against the path with a synthetic trailing `/*` appended (or drop `FNM_PATHNAME` only for a `**`-terminated glob and keep it for the rest). That closes this exact class of regression for the shipped default *and* removes a footgun for any operator who reasonably writes `/foo/**` believing it already recurses — without requiring the README, the default constant, and every downstream site config to be rewritten in the new idiom. Advisory: evaluate the redesign cost against just documenting the `**/*` idiom; not required to unblock this plan.
